<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 http://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">

  <!-- Trigger vulnerability scan -->
  <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>
  <groupId>com.tesobe</groupId>
  <artifactId>obp-parent</artifactId>
  <version>1.10.1</version>
  <packaging>pom</packaging>
  <name>Open Bank Project API Parent</name>
  <inceptionYear>2011</inceptionYear>
  <properties>
    <scala.version>2.13</scala.version>
    <scala.compiler>2.13.18</scala.compiler>
    <liquibase.version>4.29.1</liquibase.version>
    <pekko.version>1.1.5</pekko.version>
    <pekko-http.version>1.1.0</pekko-http.version>
    <!-- v1.0.5 is the first tag published for both Scala versions. Up to v1.0.4 JitPack ran its
         default `sbt clean publishM2`, which publishes only the default Scala version, so
         lift-persistence_2.13 did not exist however the tag was numbered. -->
    <lift.version>v1.0.5</lift.version>
    <http4s.version>0.23.30</http4s.version>
    <!-- Common plugin settings -->
    <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
    <project.reporting.outputEncoding>${project.build.sourceEncoding}</project.reporting.outputEncoding>
    <maven.test.failure.ignore>true</maven.test.failure.ignore>
    <!-- vscaladoc settings -->
    <maven.scaladoc.vscaladocVersion>1.2-m1</maven.scaladoc.vscaladocVersion>
    <vscaladoc.links.liftweb.pathsufix>scaladocs/</vscaladoc.links.liftweb.pathsufix>
    <vscaladoc.links.liftweb.baseurl>http://scala-tools.org/mvnsites/liftweb</vscaladoc.links.liftweb.baseurl>

    <!--java version-->
    <java.version>25</java.version>
    <maven.compiler.source>${java.version}</maven.compiler.source>
    <maven.compiler.target>${java.version}</maven.compiler.target>
    <!-- The mssql-jdbc jre suffix is part of the version string, not a classifier, and it is
         decoupled from java.version because the driver has no artifact at our Java level: 13.4.0
         ships only jre8 and jre11, and so does every other current release including the 13.5.x
         previews. Microsoft published jre17/18/20 builds for 10.2.x through 12.3.0 and then went
         back to two targets, so there is nothing newer to move to. jre11 is what runs: its classes
         are major version 55, it carries no module-info and no META-INF/versions entries, and the
         driver loads on JDK 25. Revisit only if a release appears with a jre suffix at or above
         java.version. -->
    <mssql.jre.version>11</mssql.jre.version>

    <!-- The -release scala-maven-plugin passes to scalac, and on to javac for the .java sources
         that live under src/main/scala. It bounds which JDK API our own sources may reference.

         Kept aligned with java.version: there is no supported configuration below it. The test
         runner aborts unless it finds exactly that JDK, every Dockerfile is eclipse-temurin:25,
         and every CI job sets java-version 25 - so a build host on an older JDK does not exist,
         and capping this lower would only hide APIs we are entitled to use.

         Still a separate property rather than a direct ${java.version} reference, because the two
         express different constraints. java.version is the JDK we target and ship on; this is the
         highest -release the *compiler* will accept, which is the spec version of the JDK actually
         running scalac - not a property of the Scala version. The same scalac 2.12.21 accepts up
         to 17 on JDK 17, up to 21 on JDK 21 and up to 25 on JDK 25. So whenever java.version moves
         ahead of the JDK on the build host, the two must be able to come apart; wiring this to
         ${java.version} would make that impossible to express. That is exactly what happened on
         2026-07-03: java.version had just gone to 25 while a build host was still on 21, scalac
         rejected -release 25 with "'25' is not a valid choice for '-release'", and the symptom was
         read as a Scala-version limit. maven-enforcer-plugin now fails fast on that case instead.

         On Scala 2.12 this only widened the visible API surface and did not raise the class-file
         version: 2.12 emitted Java 8 class files whatever -release said, so a Scala class calling a
         Java 25 method failed at run time with NoSuchMethodError rather than at load time with
         UnsupportedClassVersionError. Scala 2.13 honours it fully and emits class files at this
         level, so that check is now real.

         The 2.13 migration planned to hold this at 8 across the flip, so that the language version
         and the bytecode version moved in separate, separately revertible commits. That is not
         possible: the visible API surface is not decorative even on 2.12. The sources call
         String.isBlank (Java 11) in five places and java.io.ObjectInputFilter (Java 9) in
         BankAccountCreationDispatcher, where it filters deserialization - and -release 8 hides
         both, so the module does not compile. Dropping the filter to satisfy a build property
         would trade a security control for tidiness, so the two axes move together and the
         verification that belonged to the bytecode step - confirming the class-file level actually
         moved, and exercising everything that reads bytecode at run time - happens on this. -->
    <scalac.release>25</scalac.release>
  </properties>

  <modules>
    <module>obp-commons</module>
    <module>obp-api</module>
  </modules>

  <!--
    oss.sonatype.org (OSSRH) was retired. It now 302-redirects to repo1.maven.org, so the two
    repositories that pointed at it served nothing Maven Central does not already serve, and Maven
    consults Central by default: they were pure duplicates that could only add failure modes.

    One of those failure modes was real. From GitHub Actions runners the same host answers 403,
    which killed a build outright. It did so intermittently rather than always, which is what made
    it hard to attribute: Maven consults a remote repository only when a POM is not already in the
    local cache, so a warm runner never touched them and a cold one failed the whole build before a
    single test ran: the same commit passing in one CI run and failing in the next.

    Because they were listed first, Maven attributed downloads to them, and a local cache built
    before this change records artifacts against those ids in _remote.repositories. That is only a
    bookkeeping detail (both are redirects to Central, so the bytes came from Central either way),
    but it does mean an existing cache needs one online run per goal to be re-stamped before
    `mvn -o` works again. Verified by resolving, and compiling, the full reactor against an empty
    local repository with these removed.

    The two that remain are load-bearing: git-OpenBankProject serves OBP's own published artifacts,
    and jitpack.io serves the pinned lift-persistence build.
  -->
  <repositories>
    <!-- Central declared explicitly so it is searched BEFORE the repos below.
         Maven otherwise appends the super-POM's central entry after pom-declared
         repositories, which lets jitpack shadow every legitimate com.github.*
         groupId on Central: jitpack maps the coordinate to a GitHub repo, builds
         it, and answers with an sbt aggregate pom whose submodules then 401 -
         observed with com.github.dwickern:scala-nameof_2.13:4.1.0. With central
         first, jitpack is only consulted for what Central does not have
         (lift-persistence, OpenBankProject scala-macros). -->
    <repository>
      <id>central</id>
      <url>https://repo.maven.apache.org/maven2</url>
    </repository>
    <repository>
      <id>git-OpenBankProject</id>
      <name>OpenBankProject Git based repo</name>
      <url>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/OpenBankProject/OBP-M2-REPO/master/</url>
    </repository>
    <repository>
      <id>jitpack.io</id>
      <url>https://jitpack.io</url>
    </repository>
  </repositories>

  <dependencyManagement>
    <dependencies>
      <!-- Jackson BOM: forces all com.fasterxml.jackson.* 2.x artifacts to one
           version. Mixing minor versions (databind 2.12 + core 2.14 + ...) is
           unsupported and causes subtle runtime failures. -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson</groupId>
        <artifactId>jackson-bom</artifactId>
        <version>2.18.7</version>
        <type>pom</type>
        <scope>import</scope>
      </dependency>
      <!-- Force json-smart 2.6.0 to override the 2.4.7 pulled in via
           oauth2-oidc-sdk 9.27. Direct-dep override in obp-api/pom.xml did
           not take effect — using dependencyManagement here works reliably.
           Fixes CVE-2023-1370. -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>net.minidev</groupId>
        <artifactId>json-smart</artifactId>
        <version>2.6.0</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>net.minidev</groupId>
        <artifactId>accessors-smart</artifactId>
        <version>2.6.0</version>
      </dependency>
      <!-- Pin org.json to 20250107. Fixes CVE-2022-45688 and CVE-2023-5072. -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.json</groupId>
        <artifactId>json</artifactId>
        <version>20250107</version>
      </dependency>
      <!-- Pin commons-validator to 1.9.0. Fixes CVE-2020-13956 (ReDoS in URL validation). -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>commons-validator</groupId>
        <artifactId>commons-validator</artifactId>
        <version>1.9.0</version>
      </dependency>
      <!-- Force gson to 2.11.0 to override the 2.8.5 pulled in transitively by
           stripe-java 12.1.0. Fixes CVE-2022-25647 (DoS via deeply nested structures). -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.google.code.gson</groupId>
        <artifactId>gson</artifactId>
        <version>2.11.0</version>
      </dependency>
      <!-- Force Java-WebSocket to 1.5.4 to override the 1.5.3 pulled in transitively
           by web3j 4.9.8. Fixes CVE-2023-43669 (path traversal in WebSocket server). -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.java-websocket</groupId>
        <artifactId>Java-WebSocket</artifactId>
        <version>1.5.4</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.tesobe</groupId>
        <artifactId>obp-commons</artifactId>
        <version>${project.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.github.OpenBankProject.lift-persistence</groupId>
        <artifactId>lift-persistence_${scala.version}</artifactId>
        <version>${lift.version}</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>io.github.json4s</groupId>
        <artifactId>json4s-native_${scala.version}</artifactId>
        <!-- org.json4s:json4s-native_3:4.1.0-M8 (the version this comment used to pin) still
             throws "Can't find ScalaSig for class ..." extracting a Scala-3-compiled case class,
             scala3-staging on the classpath or not - measured directly against obp-api's own
             CallContextLight. The project moved to the io.github.json4s groupId after M8
             (org.json4s's last publish there is 2024-11-07); 4.1.1 under the new groupId is the
             first stable (non-milestone) release downstream of that move and is what actually
             round-trips a Scala-3-compiled case class - measured the same way. Bumped ahead of
             the flip so the 3632 extract call sites are validated by the full suite on 2.13 at
             the same version the flip will use; the flip itself only swaps the suffix to _3. -->
        <version>4.1.1</version>
      </dependency>
      <!-- https://mvnrepository.com/artifact/org.apache.commons/commons-lang3 -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.commons</groupId>
        <artifactId>commons-lang3</artifactId>
        <version>3.18.0</version>
      </dependency>
      <!-- https://mvnrepository.com/artifact/org.apache.commons/commons-text -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.apache.commons</groupId>
        <artifactId>commons-text</artifactId>
        <version>1.12.0</version>
      </dependency>
      <!-- https://mvnrepository.com/artifact/com.google.guava/guava -->
      <dependency>
        <groupId>com.google.guava</groupId>
        <artifactId>guava</artifactId>
        <version>32.0.0-jre</version>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.scalatest</groupId>
        <artifactId>scalatest_${scala.version}</artifactId>
        <!-- 3.2.20, not 3.0.8: 3.0.8 has no Scala 3 build, so the flip cannot carry it. The
             upgrade is done here on 2.13, ahead of the flip, because on 2.13 the suite still
             runs - bundling a test-framework migration into the flip would leave both
             unverifiable until every Scala 3 compile error was also fixed.
             3.2.x moved the style traits into per-style packages and removed the old names:
             org.scalatest.FlatSpec -> flatspec.AnyFlatSpec, FeatureSpec ->
             featurespec.AnyFeatureSpec, Matchers -> matchers.should.Matchers, and
             AnyFeatureSpec capitalises its DSL (feature/scenario -> Feature/Scenario). -->
        <version>3.2.20</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
      </dependency>
      <dependency>
        <groupId>org.scalactic</groupId>
        <artifactId>scalactic_${scala.version}</artifactId>
        <version>3.2.20</version>
        <scope>test</scope>
      </dependency>

    </dependencies>
  </dependencyManagement>

  <build>
    <pluginManagement>
      <plugins>
        <plugin>
          <groupId>net.alchim31.maven</groupId>
          <artifactId>scala-maven-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>4.8.1</version>
          <configuration>
            <scalaVersion>${scala.compiler}</scalaVersion>
            <charset>${project.build.sourceEncoding}</charset>
            <!-- Set explicitly because scala-maven-plugin otherwise derives -release from
                 maven.compiler.target (= ${java.version}), which would demand a JDK 25 build host.
                 See the scalac.release property for the ceiling rule and its caveats. -->
            <release>${scalac.release}</release>
            <displayCmd>true</displayCmd>
            <!-- Optimized JVM settings for faster Scala compilation -->
            <jvmArgs>
              <jvmArg>-DpackageLinkDefs=file://${project.build.directory}/packageLinkDefs.properties</jvmArg>
              <jvmArg>-Xms512m</jvmArg>
              <jvmArg>-Xmx3G</jvmArg>
              <jvmArg>-Xss4m</jvmArg>
              <jvmArg>-XX:+UseG1GC</jvmArg>
              <jvmArg>-XX:ReservedCodeCacheSize=512m</jvmArg>
              <jvmArg>-XX:+TieredCompilation</jvmArg>
            </jvmArgs>
            <!-- Optimized compiler args for speed -->
            <args>
              <arg>-unchecked</arg>
              <!-- Removed -explaintypes for faster compilation -->
              <!--
              <arg>-verbose</arg>
              <arg>-deprecation</arg>
              <arg>-explaintypes</arg>
              -->
              <!-- -Xsource:2.13 was here to compile the then-2.12 sources under 2.13 language
                   rules, so constructs changing meaning were reported before the version moved.
                   Removed with the flip: scalac describes the flag as enabling "warnings and
                   features for a future version", and 2.13 is not a future version of a 2.13
                   compiler, so it does nothing now. It never covered the collection types 2.13
                   removes outright either - only language semantics - which is why those had to
                   be found by compiling. -->
              <!-- Replaces the paradise compiler plugin; enables macro annotations on 2.13. -->
              <arg>-Ymacro-annotations</arg>
              <!-- Parallel compilation optimization -->
              <arg>-Ybackend-parallelism</arg>
              <arg>4</arg>
            </args>
            <!-- Enable incremental compilation with Zinc -->
            <recompileMode>incremental</recompileMode>
          </configuration>
          <executions>
            <execution>
              <goals>
                <goal>compile</goal>
                <goal>testCompile</goal>
              </goals>
            </execution>
          </executions>
        </plugin>
        <!-- https://mvnrepository.com/artifact/org.scalatest/scalatest-maven-plugin -->
        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.scalatest</groupId>
          <artifactId>scalatest-maven-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>2.2.0</version>
        </plugin>
      </plugins>
    </pluginManagement>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>3.5.2</version>
        <configuration>
          <skipTests>true</skipTests>
        </configuration>
      </plugin>
        <plugin>
          <groupId>net.alchim31.maven</groupId>
          <artifactId>scala-maven-plugin</artifactId>
          <configuration>
            <!-- Macro annotations were a compiler plugin on 2.12 (paradise, released per compiler
                 patch version and abandoned in 2019) and are a compiler flag on 2.13. The flag is
                 added to <args> below; there is no plugin to declare. -->
          </configuration>
        </plugin>
        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-resources-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>3.3.1</version>
          <executions>
            <execution>
              <id>default-copy-resources</id>
              <phase>process-resources</phase>
              <goals>
                <goal>copy-resources</goal>
              </goals>
              <configuration>
                <overwrite>true</overwrite>
                <outputDirectory>${project.build.directory}</outputDirectory>
                <resources>
                  <resource>
                    <directory>${project.basedir}/src</directory>
                    <includes>
                      <include>packageLinkDefs.properties</include>
                    </includes>
                    <filtering>true</filtering>
                  </resource>
                </resources>
              </configuration>
            </execution>
          </executions>
        </plugin>
        <!-- Fail fast when the build JDK is older than scalac's -release. scalac cannot target a
             release newer than the JDK running it, so without this check the build dies minutes
             later inside scala-maven-plugin with "'25' is not a valid choice for '-release'" -
             which reads as a Scala-version limit rather than a stale build host, and was misread
             that way on 2026-07-03. The bound is ${scalac.release} itself, not a literal, so the
             rule can never drift from the actual constraint.

             No custom <message>: the rule's default text is strictly more useful here, naming the
             detected version, its JAVA_HOME and the required range, e.g.
               Detected JDK version 17.0.5 (JAVA_HOME=...) is not in the allowed range [25,).
             A custom message would replace that rather than add to it. -->
        <plugin>
          <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
          <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
          <version>3.5.0</version>
          <executions>
            <execution>
              <id>enforce-build-jdk</id>
              <phase>validate</phase>
              <goals>
                <goal>enforce</goal>
              </goals>
              <configuration>
                <rules>
                  <requireJavaVersion>
                    <version>[${scalac.release},)</version>
                  </requireJavaVersion>
                </rules>
              </configuration>
            </execution>
          </executions>
        </plugin>



        <!-- The build stamp (git.properties) is produced by
             scripts/write_git_properties.sh, wired into obp-api's build. It used to
             come from git-commit-id-maven-plugin, declared here so every module got
             one; that put two git.properties on the runtime classpath and, worse,
             stamped the wrong repository for any build run from a git worktree. See
             the obp-api/pom.xml execution and the script header for the details. -->

      <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-site-plugin</artifactId>
        <version>4.0.0-M13</version>
      </plugin>

      </plugins>
  </build>
  <reporting>
    <plugins>
      <plugin>
        <groupId>net.alchim31.maven</groupId>
        <artifactId>scala-maven-plugin</artifactId>
      </plugin>
    </plugins>
  </reporting>
</project>
